FPC Suspension Strain Gauge Research:

Strain Gauges are small sensors that measures how much an object stretches or compresses. It is made on foil where it is shaped into a zig zag pattern that allows it to sense the strain.
They sit on a thin insulating material that can support the foil grid while not getting in the way.
Then at the end of the sensor are two points for electrical leads that connect to the sensor.


If the metal stretches on the sensor then it increases its resistance, whilel if it compresses then it decreases resistance.

This is done with the equation: R= p*(L/A)

Strain is measured by strain = deltaL/Original Length
So if the bar is 100mm long and it stretches by .1mm then it would be .1/100=.001

Relationship with resistance and strain:
deltaR/R = G * strain
(G = Gauge Factor)

Trace length for 350 ohms:

R= p*L/(w*t)

p = copper's resistivity at 1.68*10^-8 ohm*m (Found on this site: http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/Tables/rstiv.html)
L is the trace length
w is the trace width (3 mil = .0762mm) This is assumed on the JLC website.
t is the copper thickness = 12 micrometers
R = 350
rearrange equation for L:
L = R*w*t/p

Length with 12 micrometer of copper thickness which has a 3mil minimum width:
L = 350 * (7.62*10^-5)*(1.2*10^-5)/(1.68*10^-8) = Roughly 19.05m

